  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952589/assets/95be2e7bf1dc425e0f0dd9ba818a6a06d0c9afc7874213194f68b1dd426cb110.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ and the line $y=1.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$\n\n$$\n\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=1\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$",
